'''Red Bull Racing Limited''',<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 November 1995 |title=Red Bull Racing Limited overview - Find and update company information - Gov.uk |url=https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/03120645 |access-date=3 January 2026 |website=[[Companies House]] |language=en}}</ref> currently competing as&nbsp;'''Oracle Red Bull Racing''' and also known simply as '''Red Bull''' or '''RBR''', is a [[Formula One]] racing team, [[List of Formula One constructors#Team's nationality|competing under an Austrian racing licence]] and based in England. It is one of two Formula One teams owned by conglomerate [[Red Bull GmbH]], the other being [[Racing Bulls]]. The Red Bull Racing team was managed by [[Christian Horner]] from its formation in 2005 until 2025, when he departed the team and was replaced by [[Laurent Mekies]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.infiniti-redbullracing.com/cs/Satellite/en_INT/Red-Bull-Racing-Christian-Horner/001242969763138|title=Christian Horner: Team Principal|publisher=Infiniti Red Bull Racing|access-date=27 September 2013|url-status=usurped|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130714092438/http://www.infiniti-redbullracing.com/cs/Satellite/en_INT/Red-Bull-Racing-Christian-Horner/001242969763138|archive-date=14 July 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite tweet |url=https://x.com/F1/status/1942880588199063747 |title=Breaking: Christian Horner is to exit Red Bull Racing with immediate effect |user=F1 |author=[[Formula 1]] |number=1942880588199063747 |date=9 July 2025 |access-date=30 December 2025}}</ref>

Red Bull had [[Cosworth]] engines in {{F1|2005}} and [[Scuderia Ferrari|Ferrari]] engines in {{F1|2006}}. The team used engines supplied by [[Renault in Formula One|Renault]] between {{F1|2007}} and {{F1|2018}} (from {{F1|2016}} to 2018, the Renault engine was re-badged [[TAG Heuer#Motorsport|TAG Heuer]] following the breakdown in the relationship between Red Bull and Renault in {{F1|2015}}).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/140512/tag-heuer-stays-despite-red-bull-honda-deal|title=TAG Heuer extends Red Bull F1 deal despite 2019 Honda engine deal|first=Adam|last=Cooper|website=Autosport.com|access-date=5 June 2019|archive-date=5 June 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190605122508/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/140512/tag-heuer-stays-despite-red-bull-honda-deal|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/14855045|work=[[BBC Sport]]|publisher=BBC|date=9 September 2011|access-date=9 September 2011|title=Red Bull extend Renault engine contract to 2016|archive-date=30 June 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180630153144/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/14855045|url-status=live}}</ref> During this partnership, they won four successive Drivers' and Constructors' Championship titles in {{F1|2010}}, [[2011 Formula One World Championship|2011]], [[2012 Formula One World Championship|2012]], and {{F1|2013}}, led by Red Bull driver [[Sebastian Vettel]], and becoming the first Austrian team to win the title.<ref name="lic">{{cite web |url=http://www.fia.com/en-GB/mediacentre/pressreleases/ica/2009/Pages/ica_140409.aspx |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121117083045/http://www.fia.com/en-GB/mediacentre/pressreleases/ica/2009/Pages/ica_140409.aspx |archive-date=17 November 2012 |title= International Court of Appeal |publisher= [[FIA]] |access-date=17 November 2012}}</ref>

The team began using [[Honda in Formula One|Honda]] engines in {{F1|2019}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1121512_2019-red-bull-racing-f1-car-revealed-fires-up-honda-engine-at-silverstone|title=2019 Red Bull Racing F1 car revealed, fires up Honda engine at Silverstone|publisher=Motorauthority|author=Sean Szymkowski|date=14 February 2019 |access-date=11 June 2019|archive-date=3 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220103135832/https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1121512_2019-red-bull-racing-f1-car-revealed-fires-up-honda-engine-at-silverstone|url-status=live}}</ref> The works Honda partnership culminated in {{F1|2021}}, following Red Bull driver [[Max Verstappen]]'s World Drivers' Championship victory, with Verstappen also winning the championship in {{F1|2022}}, {{F1|2023}}, and {{F1|2024}}; he missed out to [[Lando Norris]] in {{f1|2025}}. Red Bull also won two Constructors' Championship titles in 2022 and 2023, but lost out in 2021 to [[Mercedes-Benz in Formula One|Mercedes]] and in 2024 and 2025 to [[McLaren]]. Honda left the sport officially after 2021 but continued to supply complete engines from Japan to the team partly under [[Red Bull Powertrains]] branding until the end of {{F1|2025}}.<ref name=Extend1>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-honda-and-red-bull-extend-power-unit-support-deal-until-2025.7hFIkY4W18FcmxNlWnB2ST.html|title=Honda and Red Bull extend power unit support deal until 2025|website=Formula 1|date=2 August 2022|access-date=8 April 2023|archive-date=11 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230211235156/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-honda-and-red-bull-extend-power-unit-support-deal-until-2025.7hFIkY4W18FcmxNlWnB2ST.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Following the conclusion of their partnership with Honda, Red Bull switched to [[Ford Motorsport|Ford]] engines in {{f1|2026}} after having signed a deal to use their engines in 2023.<ref name="Ford"/> Red Bull have a new [[wind tunnel]] due to be operational by 2026.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Vording |first1=Ronald |title=Red Bull ahead of schedule with new F1 wind tunnel |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/red-bull-ahead-of-schedule-with-new-f1-wind-tunnel/10686031/ |website=Motorsport.com |access-date=3 January 2025 |date=3 January 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Lawrence |first1=Dan |title=Red Bull ahead of schedule replacing 'Cold War relic' F1 wind tunnel |url=https://www.motorsportweek.com/2025/01/04/red-bull-ahead-of-schedule-replacing-cold-war-relic-f1-wind-tunnel/ |website=Motorsport Week |access-date=4 January 2025 |date=4 January 2025}}</ref>

