The history of the [[Jews]] in [[Germany]] goes back at least to the year 321 AD,<ref>{{Cite web|title=1700 years of Jewish life in Germany|url=http://www.bmi.bund.de/SharedDocs/pressemitteilungen/EN/2021/01/1700-years-jewish-life-in-germany.html;jsessionid=A71DFBE16A62F57E2A08432D6DD628FA.1_cid287?nn=9384552|access-date=21 February 2021|website=Federal Ministry of the Interior, Building and Community|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=German Jewish culture through the ages|url=https://www.dw.com/en/celebrating-1700-years-of-jewish-life-in-germany/a-56131698|access-date=21 February 2021|website=amp.dw.com}}</ref> and continued through the [[Early Middle Ages]] (5th to 10th centuries AD) and [[High Middle Ages]] (c. 1000–1299 AD) when [[Jews|Jewish]] immigrants from France founded the [[Ashkenazi Jews|Ashkenazi Jewish]] community. The community survived under [[Charlemagne]], but suffered during the [[Crusades]]. Accusations of [[well poisoning]] during the [[Black Death]] (1346–1353) led to mass slaughter of German Jews,<ref>{{cite book |last= Benedictow |first=Ole Jørgen |year=2004 |title=The Black Death, 1346–1353: The Complete History |publisher= Boydell Press |isbn=1-84383-214-3 |pages=392–93 |url= https://books.google.com/books?id=KjLHAOE7irsC&q=Black%20Death%201346%20German%20Jews&pg=PA393 |access-date=1 October 2012 }}</ref> while others [[History of the Jews in Poland|fled in large numbers to Poland]]. The Jewish communities of the cities of [[Mainz]], [[Speyer]] and [[Worms, Germany|Worms]] became the center of Jewish life during medieval times. "This was a [[golden age]] as area bishops protected the Jews, resulting in increased trade and prosperity."<ref name=Mainz>{{cite web |title=The Magic Land of Magenza: Jewish Life and Times in Medieval and Modern Mainz |website= Mainz.de| publisher= City of Mainz |url= http://mainz.de/WGAPublisher/online/html/default/hthn-5vgjlb.en.html |url-status=dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20131113210512/http://mainz.de/WGAPublisher/online/html/default/hthn-5vgjlb.en.html |archive-date=13 November 2013}}</ref>

The [[First Crusade]] began an era of persecution of Jews in Germany.{{r|Riley-Smith1991}} Entire communities, like those of [[Trier]], Worms, Mainz and [[Cologne]], were slaughtered. The [[Hussite Wars]] became the signal for renewed persecution of Jews. The end of the 15th century was a period of religious hatred that ascribed to Jews all possible evils. With Napoleon's fall in 1815, growing [[German nationalism|nationalism]] resulted in increasing repression. From August to October 1819, [[pogrom]]s that came to be known as the [[Hep-Hep riots]] took place throughout Germany. During this time, many [[States of Germany|German states]] stripped Jews of their civil rights. As a result, many German Jews began to emigrate.

From the time of [[Moses Mendelssohn]] until the 20th century, the community gradually achieved [[Jewish emancipation|emancipation]], and then prospered.<ref name="bh.org.il">{{cite book |title=The Jews of Germany |publisher=The Museum of the Jewish People at Beit Hatfutsot}}</ref>

In January 1933, roughly 530,000 Jews lived in Germany.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Jewish Population of Europe in 1933: Population Data by Country |url=https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jewish-population-of-europe-in-1933-population-data-by-country#central-europe-1 |access-date=2025-08-09 |website=Holocaust Encyclopedia |language=en}}</ref> After the [[Adolf Hitler's rise to power|Nazis took power]] and implemented their [[Antisemitism|antisemitic]] ideology and policies, the Jewish community was increasingly persecuted. About 60% (numbering around 306,000) emigrated during the first six years of the [[Nazi Germany|Nazi dictatorship]]. In 1933, persecution of the Jews became an official [[Nazi Party|Nazi]] policy. In 1935 and 1936, the pace of antisemitic persecution increased. In 1936, Jews were banned from all professional jobs, effectively preventing them from participating in education, politics, higher education and industry. On 9 November 1938, the state police and Nazi paramilitary forces orchestrated the Night of Broken Glass (''[[Kristallnacht]]''), in which the storefronts of Jewish shops and offices were smashed and vandalized, and many synagogues were destroyed by fire. Only roughly 214,000 Jews were left in Germany proper (1937 borders) on the eve of [[World War II]].{{r|DeJewsUSHMM}}

Beginning in late 1941, the remaining community was subjected to systematic [[Holocaust trains|deportations]] to ghettos and, ultimately, to [[death camps]] in [[Eastern Europe]].<ref name= DeJewsUSHMM>{{cite web |title=German Jews During The Holocaust, 1939–1945 |website= USHMM.org| publisher= [[United States Holocaust Memorial Museum]] |url= http://www.ushmm.org/wlc/article.php?lang=en&ModuleId=10005469 |access-date= 1 October 2012 }}</ref> In May 1943, Germany was declared ''[[judenrein]]'' (clean of Jews; also ''judenfrei'': free of Jews).{{r|DeJewsUSHMM}} By the end of the war, an estimated 160,000 to 180,000 German Jews had been killed by the [[Nazi Germany|Nazi regime]] and their collaborators.{{r|DeJewsUSHMM}} A total of about six million European Jews were murdered under the direction of the Nazis, in the genocide that later came to be known as [[the Holocaust]].

After the war, the Jewish community in Germany started to slowly grow again. Beginning around 1990, a spurt of growth was fueled by immigration from the [[Post-Soviet states|former Soviet Union]], so that at the turn of the 21st century, Germany had the only growing Jewish community in Europe,<ref name=Welt041118>{{cite news |last=Schoelkopf |first=Katrin |title=Rabbiner Ehrenberg: Orthodoxes jüdisches Leben ist wieder lebendig in Berlin |trans-title=Rabbi Ehrenberg: Orthodox Jewish life is alive again in Berlin |newspaper=[[Die Welt]] |language=de |url=https://www.welt.de/print-welt/article353306/Rabbiner_Ehrenberg_Orthodoxes_juedisches_Leben_ist_wieder_lebendig_in_Berlin.html |date=18 November 2004}}</ref> and the majority of German Jews were [[Russian language|Russian]]-speaking. By 2018, the Jewish population of Germany had leveled off at 126,000, not including non-Jewish members of households; the total estimated enlarged population of Jews living in Germany, including non-Jewish household members, was close to 225,000.<ref name="DellaPergola" />

By German law, [[Holocaust denial|denial of the Holocaust]] or that six million Jews were murdered in the Holocaust (§ 130 StGB) is a criminal act; violations can be punished with up to five years of prison.<ref name= DW051223>{{cite news |date=23 December 2005 |url=http://www.dw-world.de/dw/article/0,2144,1833619,00.html |title=No Room for Holocaust Denial in Germany |website= dw-world.de | agency=[[Deutsche Welle]] }}</ref> In 2006, on the occasion of the [[2006 FIFA World Cup|World Cup]] held in Germany, the then-[[Interior Minister of Germany]] [[Wolfgang Schäuble]], urged vigilance against [[Far-right politics|far-right extremism]], saying: "We will not tolerate any form of extremism, [[xenophobia]], or [[antisemitism]]."<ref name= BBC060522>{{cite news |date = 22 May 2006 |title=Germans warned of neo-Nazi surge |agency=BBC News |url = https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/5005472.stm |access-date=1 June 2007 }}</ref> In spite of Germany's measures against these groups and antisemites, a number of incidents have occurred in recent years.

